{"title":"2012 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A square region <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is externally tangent to the circle with equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/d/f1dd4184b561ea747be652fc3dee7fc5e1c5103f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/1/be11a56a55425d6c6b69f70f5fa98eab95e8ad18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> on the side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />.  Vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are on the circle with equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/8/7a8b84ca3393ce6840ab42fe8edc44524e3c67e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />.  What is the side length of this square?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/5/4b588cafc447cba8405c7cdcaa13dd371f868a08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+5}{10}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{19}-4}{5}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{9-\\sqrt{17}}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"591\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/b/17b54bd31c2906599eee882f7b6902279c53365e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(15mm); defaultpen(linewidth(.8pt)+fontsize(10pt)); dotfactor=4;  real a=1; real b=2; pair O=(0,0); pair A=(-(sqrt(19)-2)/5,1); pair B=((sqrt(19)-2)/5,1); pair C=((sqrt(19)-2)/5,1+2(sqrt(19)-2)/5); pair D=(-(sqrt(19)-2)/5,1+2(sqrt(19)-2)/5); pair E=(-(sqrt(19)-2)/5,0); path inner=Circle(O,a); path outer=Circle(O,b); draw(outer); draw(inner); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);  draw(O--D--E--cycle);  label(&quot;$A$&quot;,D,NW); label(&quot;$E$&quot;,E,SW); label(&quot;$O$&quot;,O,SE); label(&quot;$s+1$&quot;,(D--E),W); label(&quot;$\\frac{s}{2}$&quot;,(E--O),S);  pair[] ps={A,B,C,D,E,O}; dot(ps); [/asy]\" width=\"285\" height=\"298\" /></center>\n<p>The circles have radii of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Draw the triangle shown in the figure above and write expressions in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> (length of the side of the square) for the sides of the triangle. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c53cc93aa43cc55bea0dc2511833fe6bdda020f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> is the radius of the larger circle, which is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we can write the Pythagorean Theorem.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/2/dd292321886aabfb5f25a00608daf9cdbbf10c26.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\left( \\frac{s}{2} \\right) ^2 + (s+1)^2 &amp;= 2^2\\\\ \\frac14 s^2 + s^2 + 2s + 1 &amp;= 4\\\\ \\frac54 s^2 +2s - 3 &amp;= 0\\\\ 5s^2 + 8s - 12 &amp;=0 \\end{align*}\" width=\"175\" height=\"146\" />\n</p><p>Use the quadratic formula.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/d/7ad6fcfea1fb8fd86a81479e331c734c0c97f65e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[s = \\frac{-8+\\sqrt{8^2-4(5)(-12)}}{10} = \\frac{-8+\\sqrt{304}}{10} = \\frac{-8+4\\sqrt{19}}{10} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{19}-4}{5}}\\]\" width=\"598\" height=\"53\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using the diagram above, we look at the top-right vertex of the square. Let us call this point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" />. Then, we that since the square is symmetrical over the y-axis, that the y value is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/8/6f8fc82e121c01034fef87ac28f73e7d4c0b465c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />, since we can multiply the x value(which is half of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />) by two to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, and we add one since the square lies one unit above the origin. Now, all we must do is find the intersection of the larger circle, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/2/b02ae718756895bc1924d5290f0abd320bfa77ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + y^2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />, and the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/c/28cd6af3919e1c83ef4d64def64db549f5b37a01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=2x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"83\" height=\"15\" />. Substituting the second equation into the first, we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/f/40fb4749aaaae9ab63da7e6c64e54d88b6bc61c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5x^2 +4x -3 = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"132\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Using the quadratic formula, we arrive with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/d/badfef1a2ae039582c470309eb782137d71c3b5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{-4 \\pm 2\\sqrt{19}}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"125\" height=\"41\" />. However, recall that the x value is only one half of the side length. Multiplying this value by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, then, and using only the positive root(since the top right vertex of the square has a positive x value), we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/b/19b25fa0a37b5f3aec6ffead763ee999a715b30d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{-4 + 2\\sqrt{19}}{5} \\Rightarrow \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"161\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like the previous solutions, we can say that the square is symmetrical with respect to the y-axis.\n</p>\n<pre>Since CD passes through point (0,1), let C be (-x,1) and D be (x,1)\n</pre>\n<p>Point B is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/c/75c715ca3c7dc75557b0dd687d9026605666fc08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(-x, \\sqrt{4-x^2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"115\" height=\"32\" /> and Point A is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/b/40b8b93add32c7cc8edce67889dcd7ce358d8e54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(x,\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"101\" height=\"32\" />. Now, we see that AB = 2x and BC = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/1/1b1092069a120f9478691f300e23c2f6acd16d21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{4-x^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" />-1\n</p><p>Setting those two equal, we get the same equation as the previous solutions: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/f/40fb4749aaaae9ab63da7e6c64e54d88b6bc61c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5x^2 +4x -3 = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"132\" height=\"16\" /> and then solve the same way.\n</p><p><br />\n-Conantwiz2023\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A\" title=\"2012 AMC 12A\">2012 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?titl